{
	title: 'Paste markers and pages that show where the file lives',
	description: "A long text paste becomes a marker shaped like an image marker: [paste/<name>.txt] (the user's example: [paste/0005.txt]; the name is chosen by the client like image names, so the marker is final from the start), and the text lives on the host at /tmp/hal/paste/<name>.txt (the same place rules as images: tools can read it, the session keeps a blob copy on submit, tests use the home's tmp). The model receives the text as it does today. Clicking an image or paste marker, in the terminal (OSC 8 with a code, task e3) or on the web, opens a page on the host, /image/<name> or /paste/<name>, that shows the content (the image, or the text in a monospace block) under a header naming where the file really is on the host (the /tmp path, and the session blob path once the prompt was sent), so it is obvious which file to open in one's own tools; the raw bytes have their own address that the page uses. While an upload is in flight the marker may look slightly different (for example dimmed and not yet a link) and becomes the normal marker when the host acknowledges it; no '[uploading ...]' text is ever shown. Old [paste <blob>, N lines] markers in history still display and replay. The page URL drops the file extension (/image/<six-character id> or /paste/<id>), while old extensionful URLs keep working and /raw/<name.ext> still serves the typed bytes. A page with an ambiguous id shared by more than one file is not guessed. The page shows a high-contrast Open original link and a separate Download link with the real filename; for images it shows format, pixel dimensions and size (B or kB/MB rounded down to one decimal). Malformed or truncated image headers omit dimensions, without hiding the file. A small standalone script under scripts/ reads supported PNG, JPEG, GIF and WebP headers only when an image page is opened, not on host startup; no image library or platform utility is required. Tests: a pasted long text yields the final marker at once, the file exists at the stated path, the page names that path and shows the text, and an in-flight marker changes only its look.",
	status: 'done',
	needs: ['e3'],
	foldInto: ['qy', '2a'],
	notes: [
		"Names and markers: attachments.fileName (6 base36 + png|jpg|gif|webp|txt) and fileMarker, whose group 1 is the marker path (image/<name> or paste/<name>); ansi.links and web view.links strip its extension for the page href. attachments.named(name) is the marker. uploads.begin gives every upload a name, text/plain too, so the placeholder is the final marker and settle's swap is a no-op on success; it still swaps a refusal's error text.",
		'Host: attach with a name always goes to blobs.stage (text/plain included) into paths.fileDir(name): <tmpDir>/paste for .txt, imageDir for images; tmpDir is /tmp/hal, or <home>/tmp under HAL_HOME or tests. Without a name blobs.store still makes a 12-hex blob with the old [paste <blob>, N lines] marker, so old clients and history replay unchanged (history holds the pasted text anyway, never the marker).',
		"Pages live in host/file-page.ts (dimensions come from scripts/image-dimensions.ts on demand): /image/<id> and /paste/<id> (also legacy extensionful addresses) are HTML (no-store, CSP default-src 'none', body class page, header class info) listing the /tmp path while it exists and every sessions/*/blobs/<name> copy (blobs.file scans all sessions); /raw/<name> is the bytes (immutable cache). The page kind must match the file type, else 404. All three are file addresses for web.fetch's gate and ?auth= redirect.",
		'In flight: uploads.inFlight(marker) is true until the host answers; ansi.links draws such a marker dim and unlinked. The prompt is rebuilt on every event (app.show after attached), so it turns into a link without a text change. The web composer is a textarea and cannot style a marker, so on the web an in-flight marker looks the same (it is never a link there either).',
		"Not checked by hand in a real browser yet: the page's look (colours from web.css(), monospace <pre> wrapping) at phone and desktop widths."
	]
}
